      sources: "Sources: Learn, ms-Kds-Prov-RootKey class; Setting up dMSAs; Manage gMSAs. Chapter 06, “Goal and audience,” “The separate write path.”",
      say: `Who's involved? Developers build the code. Server and directory engineers run the write path and change the hosts. Application owners name who owns each service principal name and when it ends, and the security officer reviews the island list. The rights split is the important part. The read task holds no domain administration at all, just a narrow delegated read of the root key's dates. The write task does need administrator rights, because Learn's procedure requires them, and that's exactly why it's a separate script, run by a person, on a plan someone else approved.`,
      detail: `Audience: developers who build the project's Active Directory code, the server and Active Directory engineers who run the write path and change the hosts, the application owners who name an SPN owner and an end date, and the ISSO who reviews the island list. You should read PowerShell 7 and know Chapter 01's register. You don't need a domain until Lab 2. kerberos-island (read): Get-ADObject (KDS root keys), Get-ADServiceAccount, Get-ADUser, Get-ADDomainController, Get-ADComputer; the default read of an authenticated domain user, plus a delegated read of msKds-UseStartTime and msKds-CreateTime in CN=Master Root Keys, whose class's default security descriptor grants Enterprise Admins, Domain Admins and SYSTEM only (research gaps, rows 52 and 53). kerberos-apply (write, a person): the reads, New-ADServiceAccount, Set-ADServiceAccount, Start- and Complete-ADServiceAccountMigration, Disable-ADAccount; Domain Admins or Enterprise Admins, or equivalent, to create or migrate to a dMSA; Domain Admins or a delegated right to create gMSAs; write of msDS-GroupMSAMembership; a delegated right to disable accounts in the account's OU. The read task holds no domain administration. The write task does, because Learn's dMSA procedure needs it: that is why it is a separate script, run by a person, on a plan someone else approved.`,
