      say: `Lab 2 moves to a test domain, never production, with a Windows Server 2025 domain controller and member server. You make two test accounts, one for each path, and grant the reader only a narrow delegated read of the root key's dates, recording whether that really works without exposing the key. A second person reviews the plan and hands you its hash. You apply it as a person, then run the next plan to start the migration, restart only the test service, and watch the Kerberos events. After twenty-eight days, complete it, or back it out by hand.`,
      detail: `Use a test Active Directory domain, never production, with at least one Windows Server 2025 domain controller, a Windows Server 2025 member server, and Chapter 01's Lab 2 run (out/inventory-test). The KDS root key must already exist; in a test domain with one domain controller, Learn's KDS page shows how to create one usable at once. 1 Make two test accounts: a traditional test service account (svc- prefix) with an SPN, running a test Windows service on the 2025 member server, and a test MSA. Add both to kerberos-dispositions.csv: the first Convert to a dMSA on that host, the second Convert to a gMSA, each with a test SPN owner and an end date before your test D08. Add the gMSA's retrievers to password-retrievers.json and a test end-state.json. 2 Grant the read: give the account that runs the read a delegated read of msKds-UseStartTime and msKds-CreateTime on CN=Master Root Keys,CN=Group Key Distribution Service,CN=Services,CN=Configuration,<forest>, nothing else, and record whether the read works without access to the key data (research gaps, row 52). Record whether PrincipalsAllowedToRetrieveManagedPassword comes back for that account (row 53). 3 Run the check: ./Invoke-NpiKerberosIsland.ps1 -Cloud gcc -RegisterRun out/inventory-test -InputPath <inputs> -Server <test-dc> -OutputPath out/kerberos-test. Expect CreateDmsa and CreateGmsa. Have a second person review kerberos-plan.json and pass you its SHA-256. 4 Dry-run, then apply, signed in to the domain as a person with the test domain's Domain Admins or equivalent rights, against a writable domain controller, with -ApprovedSha256 <sha> -Server <test-dc>, first without and then with -Apply. 5 Run the next plan: the check now plans StartDmsaMigration for the dMSA and AwaitHostChange for the gMSA. Review, apply, record MigrationStartedOn. On the member server, set the host's registry value and restart only the test service, as Learn's procedure does for all services; watch Kerberos events 307 to 309 in the Security-Kerberos Operational log. Record whether the dMSA's state is 1 without first setting it to 3 (row 51). 6 Complete, or back out. After 28 days the check plans CompleteDmsaMigration; apply it and confirm the old account is disabled and its SPNs are on the dMSA. To back out instead, run Learn's Undo-ADServiceAccountMigration by hand (this code never runs it), re-enable the test account, then remove the test dMSA, gMSA, MSA and account by hand in the test domain, and delete the out folders.`,
