      sources: "Sources: Chapter 06, “Dangers”; Learn, Create a KDS root key; dMSA overview; Setting up dMSAs.",
      say: `Each of these failures has a guard in the code. The biggest is the one the chapter is built around: an account left behind when A D retires, and the guard is the reconciliation, rerun whenever the end-state decision changes. Others are about the migration itself: completing too early, on a host that can't use the new account, or deleting the old one so there's no way back. Then the quieter ones: reading the root key as a secret, widening retrievers by hand, or a plan that changed after review. Each guard is a rule that runs every time.`,
      detail: `A gMSA left behind at G5: the workload loses its credential when the last domain controller is demoted; guard: every gMSA and dMSA reconciled with D11 and G5 (NPI-KRB-07); the run repeated when D11 changes. A dMSA migration completed too early, or on a host that can't use it: the old account is disabled; the service fails on hosts the dMSA hasn't learned; guard: complete only 28 days after the start; Windows Server 2025 on every host and one domain controller; no unconstrained delegation (NPI-KRB-04). The old account deleted: no way back; the service and the dMSA link to it; guard: no delete, uninstall, undo or reset call exists; the writer refuses them by name. A root key used before it replicates: gMSA password retrieval fails on other domain controllers; guard: creations wait until a key has been usable for 10 hours (NPI-KRB-01); the code never creates a key. The root key read as a secret: whoever runs the check holds the input to every gMSA password; guard: only msKds-UseStartTime and msKds-CreateTime are read, with a delegated read. Retrievers widened by hand: another host can read the gMSA's password and act as the service; guard: retrievers are reviewed data; drift is a finding and a reviewed SetRetrievers. A restart of every service: Learn's step restarts unrelated services on the host; guard: host steps are the server owner's, scoped to the service, never run by this code. A plan changed after review: different writes from the ones approved; guard: the approved SHA-256; this tenant and domain; no older than seven days. Domain administration held by a job: a standing credential that can create and migrate any account; guard: the write path runs as a person; the read path holds no administration. An island with no owner or end: SPNs nobody retires; P4.6 has no list to work from; guard: NPI-KRB-05 and -06; the island list is ready for hand-off only when every row is.`,
